================================================================
GAME DESIGN DOCUMENT
Collaborative NPC Extension — Top-Down Shooter / Arena
================================================================


OVERVIEW
--------
Genre:          Top-down shooter / arena
New feature:    Companion NPC (fight + guide)
Build time:     1-2 sessions
Scope strategy: Extend the existing project — do not rebuild

Concept:
The player now has a Companion NPC that travels alongside them.
The companion has two roles:
  - Guide: uses A* to lead the player toward a hidden objective
  - Fighter: uses a Neural Network to decide when to fire arrows

All NPCs (enemies and companion) gain the ability to fire arrows.
Companion arrows help the player; enemy arrows punish the player
for ignoring the companion's guidance.

Teaching goals this links to:
  - A* navigation
  - Neural Networks
  - Primary / Secondary control
  - Composition in Godot
  - Reinforcement Learning (optional stretch)


================================================================
COMPANION NPC — AI DESIGN
================================================================

STATE MACHINE (Primary control)
--------------------------------
States:
  [Guide mode] --> (enemy nearby) --> [Combat mode]
  [Combat mode] --> (enemy dead / far) --> [Guide mode]

Guide mode:
  - Companion stays 80-120px ahead of the player on the A* path
  - Emits a visual signal (glow or arrow indicator) pointing
    toward the objective
  - Does not fire arrows in this state

Combat mode:
  - Companion stops moving
  - Faces the nearest threatening enemy
  - NN decides whether to fire an arrow


NAVIGATION (A*)
---------------
  - Companion runs A* on the existing tilemap
  - Recalculates path every 0.5s, or when the player moves
    more than 2 tiles
  - Picks a waypoint 2-3 tiles ahead of the player as its
    follow target — so it always appears to be leading
  - The objective tile is hidden from the player's minimap
    but known to the companion's pathfinder
  - This creates the "follow me" dynamic without a UI arrow


NEURAL NETWORK — When to fire (Secondary control)
--------------------------------------------------
Inputs (5 neurons):
  1. Distance to enemy       (normalised 0-1)
  2. Enemy health            (normalised 0-1)
  3. Player health           (normalised 0-1)
  4. Companion health        (normalised 0-1)
  5. Line of sight           (binary: 0 or 1)

Hidden layer:
  - 4-6 neurons
  - Activation: ReLU

Output (1 neuron):
  - Fire probability
  - Activation: Sigmoid (output range 0-1)
  - Fire threshold: 0.65

Teaching note:
  Students can pre-train the NN with hand-labelled data
  ("should the companion fire in this situation?") and observe
  how changing the threshold changes behaviour. This directly
  connects the backpropagation and training data lessons.


================================================================
ARROW SYSTEM — ALL NPCs
================================================================

SHARED ARROW SCENE (Godot Composition)
---------------------------------------
Scene structure:
  Arrow.tscn
    Area2D
      Sprite2D
      CollisionShape2D

Behaviour:
  - Moves in its facing direction at a fixed speed
  - Deals damage on collision
  - Calls queue_free() after 2 seconds or on first hit

Both enemy NPCs and the companion instance this same scene via
preload() + instantiate(). This is a direct application of the
Composition in Godot lesson — one scene, many users.


FIRING RULES PER ACTOR
-----------------------
Enemy NPCs:
  - Fire on a simple cooldown timer
  - No NN — keeps enemies simple and makes the companion's
    AI feel smarter by comparison

Companion NPC:
  - Fires only when NN output >= 0.65
  - AND line of sight to enemy is clear
  - Never fires if the arrow path would cross the player


FRIENDLY FIRE GUARD
--------------------
Use Godot collision layers:
  Layer 1 — Player
  Layer 2 — Companion arrows   (cannot damage player)
  Layer 3 — Enemy arrows       (cannot damage companion)

Arrow scenes check their owner's layer before dealing damage.
Students implement this — good lesson in Godot physics layers.


================================================================
OBJECTIVE & WIN CONDITION
================================================================

THE OBJECTIVE
-------------
A hidden tile (chest, portal, or flag — student's choice) is
placed in a corner of the arena. The player cannot see it on
the map at the start. The companion knows where it is and its
A* path leads there.

The player must trust and follow the companion. Enemy NPCs
spawn between the player and the objective, pressuring the
player to let the companion fight while they move.


WIN / LOSE CONDITIONS
---------------------
Win:      Player reaches the objective tile while the
          companion is still alive.

Lose (1): Player health reaches 0.

Lose (2): Companion health reaches 0 — the player loses
          their guide and cannot find the objective.

Design note:
  Losing the companion as a lose condition is the key design
  choice. It forces the player to protect the AI, making the
  collaboration feel meaningful and not just cosmetic.


================================================================
SESSION PLAN
================================================================

SESSION 1 — Companion skeleton + arrows
-----------------------------------------
1. Create Arrow.tscn (shared scene)
   - Give it to enemy NPCs first so it works immediately
2. Create Companion.tscn (CharacterBody2D + A* component)
3. Wire companion to follow player using path waypoint logic
4. Add Guide / Combat state switch based on enemy proximity
5. Companion fires arrows in Combat mode (hardcoded threshold)

End of session goal:
  Arrows fly, companion follows, basic guide behaviour works.


SESSION 2 — NN integration + polish
--------------------------------------
1. Replace hardcoded threshold with NN firing decision
2. Students label 20-30 training scenarios (spreadsheet/JSON)
3. Train NN, export weights, plug into companion
4. Add companion health bar + lose condition
5. Place hidden objective tile, test full win/lose loop
6. Show to other students — collect feedback

End of session goal:
  Fully playable, shareable demo.


STRETCH GOALS (if time allows)
--------------------------------
- Let the companion's NN weights improve via simple
  reinforcement: reward if arrow hits, penalise if missed.
  This is ~20 lines of code and directly previews next year's
  deeper RL content.

- Add a second companion with a different NN personality
  (aggressive vs conservative threshold) and let the player
  choose which to follow.



